            <div class="prose max-w-none" x-show="lang === 'en'" x-cloak>
                <p>A cross-border homeware team recently launched a magnetic phone stand on TikTok Shop. During the first three days, it prepared only three creatives: a rotating product display, a lifestyle demonstration, and an unboxing video from a creator. The videos attracted some views, but click-through and add-to-cart rates remained weak. The advertising account also ran low on funds, forcing the team to recharge while urgently producing more content.</p>
<p>This is an anonymized case, and the figures have been presented in ranges. The main problem was not simply a lack of creatives. The team had no system for building and continuously refreshing a creative pool.</p>
<h2>1. Identify the actual bottleneck</h2>
<p>The team reviewed the full funnel and separated performance into four stages:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Strong views but weak clicks:</strong> The first three seconds did not demonstrate the magnetic function, so viewers did not understand the product benefit.</li>
<li><strong>Adequate clicks but weak add-to-cart performance:</strong> The product page emphasized appearance rather than the space-saving use case, creating a mismatch with the ad promise.</li>
<li><strong>Strong add-to-cart but weak conversions:</strong> Price, promotions, and delivery expectations reduced purchase intent.</li>
<li><strong>Limited creative reuse:</strong> Each video focused on one selling point and could only be modified through repeated editing.</li>
</ul>
<p>The conclusion was clear: the bottleneck lay in attention capture and message matching, not a shortage of advertising budget. Scaling the original three creatives would only consume funds faster.</p>
<h2>2. Build the pool by breaking down selling points</h2>
<p>Instead of producing 30 unrelated videos, the team first identified five demonstrable benefits: quick installation, magnetic holding strength, multi-angle rotation, reduced desk usage, and suitability for work and travel scenarios.</p>
<p>The initial pool contained 39 creatives:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>12 product demonstrations</strong> that showed the magnetic or rotating action immediately;</li>
<li><strong>9 problem-comparison videos</strong> contrasting the product with a conventional stand;</li>
<li><strong>8 lifestyle videos</strong> featuring real use in offices, kitchens, and live-streaming setups;</li>
<li><strong>6 creator-led videos</strong> focused on easy installation and space efficiency;</li>
<li><strong>4 response videos</strong> answering questions about price, viewing angles, and stability.</li>
</ol>
<p>Every creative was linked to one selling point, one use case, and one call to action. A consistent naming convention connected the asset to its account, campaign, and performance data. This allowed the team to reuse winning combinations even when the creator or ad account changed.</p>
<h2>3. Test continuously instead of waiting for production to finish</h2>
<p>The team used controlled parallel testing rather than launching one or two creatives at a time. The first rounds evaluated watch time, clicks, and add-to-cart behavior. Low view counts alone did not justify pausing a creative, while promising videos advanced to conversion testing.</p>
<p>The account structure also separated functions. A primary account carried validated combinations, while testing accounts handled new hooks and creator content. Recharges were scheduled according to daily spending, available balance, and the next day's planned delivery. Balance alerts helped prevent a promising campaign from being interrupted by insufficient funds.</p>
<h2>4. Refresh the pool through retirement and replication</h2>
<p>The team did not apply a single performance threshold to every creative. Videos with weak delivery were revised at the opening, videos with strong clicks but weak add-to-cart rates were realigned with the product page, and videos with healthy add-to-cart but weak conversion rates triggered a review of pricing and promotions.</p>
<p>After the first test cycle, 11 of the 39 creatives entered the scalable group, while only one of the original three remained active. Rather than rebuilding the entire pool, the team derived 24 new assets by changing creators, scenarios, and proof moments around the winning structures. Testing costs declined, and the account became less dependent on a small number of accidental hits.</p>
<h2>5. Integrate reconciliation and risk control</h2>
<p>Creative IDs, accounts, campaigns, spending, and recharge batches were recorded in the same system. The team compared platform invoices, payment records, and account balances daily and reviewed authorization settings whenever abnormal consumption appeared.</p>
<p>For businesses operating multiple accounts, Yiyu Digital Media provides centralized support for account setup, recharging, balance alerts, and multi-account operations. This does not automatically improve creative performance. Its value is operational: fewer interruptions, fewer duplicate recharges, and clearer reconciliation between advertising spend and the creatives responsible for it.</p>
<h2>The real advantage is validation speed</h2>
<p>A TikTok Shop cold start should begin by defining selling points and producing multiple ways to express them. The team should validate the critical funnel stages before scaling budgets and immediately create derivatives when a winner appears. When the creative pool, funding workflow, and reconciliation process are connected, a new product can generate reusable, trackable, and testable advertising assets with greater budget discipline.</p>
